## Start here and next implementation step

Start at src/SimpleATH.cpp, not at a disconnected unit test. The native
PCI/RTC/NVM/keycache, EDMA queue, and CCMP/EAPOL packet paths now have
production call sites, but the radio/connection owner is incomplete. In the
current native source, `radioOperational_` and `channelAuthorized_` begin
false and are only cleared on teardown; no successful startup/scan/tune path
sets either true. The existing source-backed
`runAr9565ReceiveOnlyRadioSequence` (INI, PHY mode, board modal, RF bus,
PLL, synthesizer, OFDM timing, calibration) is not called by the native
controller, and is RX-only even when it succeeds. The driver must not
replace missing calibrated/channel/regulatory operations with asserted flags.
An end-to-end integrated, authorized driver is therefore still missing.

The native association owner now schedules a separate same-workloop beacon
watchdog after an actual AP association response. It polls the portable
`WifiEngine::checkBeaconTimeout` at one-second intervals and routes an expired
link through the existing native controlled-port, software CCMP and key-epoch
revocation path used for AP deauthentication/disassociation. Failed timer
scheduling, lost RF/queue ownership, explicit disconnect, sleep and teardown
also invalidate the watchdog's epoch/association generation; scan timing stays
independent. The `test_wifi_engine` fixture checks the portable deadline and
one-shot link-loss action. This is **source-only**: the board-acquisition-only
boot still cannot arm the watchdog in practice because RF/channel startup and
native packet queues remain unverified; it does not prove hardware keycache
scrub, interrupt service, reconnection or physical Wi-Fi operation.

### Prior link-loss audit (2026-11-11)

Before the guarded quarantine transition described below,
`SimpleATH::retireNativeLink` revoked software PTK/GTK CCMP and watchdog
state but left EDMA, automatic MAC responses, hardware keycache and portable
station/scan state alive. The existing `nativeRemoveKeys` operated under a
separate key-update lease, and `stopHardware` could wipe hardware keys only
after queue isolation. Neither function was safe to invoke from an arbitrary
RX callback without a same-epoch, quiesced hardware owner. Software-only
revoke could not authorize another association in that PCI epoch.

The following implementation closes this immediate isolation/wipe admission
gap, but does not yet provide a successful, evidence-backed physical reset
and reconnect coordinator. It deliberately denies same-epoch reconnection.

### Native link-loss quarantine (source implementation, 2026-11-11)

The command-gated `retireNativeLink` now routes verified AP disconnects,
beacon timeout, terminal RX security denial and a successfully queued local
leave through one software revoke and hardware queue-retirement path. It
immediately closes the controlled data port, invalidates CCMP/PN receive
state, attempts to mask IRQ, disable bus mastering and quiesce the MAC, and
quarantines the old EDMA queue without recycling a borrowed RX buffer or
resuming pending DMA. Only a verified same-epoch queue isolation **with no
outstanding TX descriptor** permits a conditional keycache scrub using a
separate, narrowly scoped hardware write permit. Erasure failure, pending
TX, a lost PCI epoch or failed isolation cannot publish a wipe; the old
hardware epoch always remains reset-required. The portable engine's scan,
station, supplicant, fragment and replay state is reset after this transition.
Old queued transmit completions and scan results cannot authorize reconnect.

`tests/test_native_wireless_epoch.cpp` exercises callback order, failed queue
isolation, stale physical/key epochs, outstanding device-owned TX,
a missing callback context, skipped wipe when uninitialized, and failed erasure
with fake callbacks. This is **not** a device measurement: native Tahoe build,
real MAC/EDMA quiescence and physical key RAM verification are untested.
Automatic same-epoch reconnect is intentionally denied, not implemented;
a new PCI/reset/calibration/channel/scan/key epoch is still required. No
native asynchronous reset/reconnect coordinator exists yet. Keep this task
open until an end-to-end, correctly owned recovery path is verified.

A follow-on source fix also routes failed protected M4/Group2 EDMA
completions, failed deferred protected replies, and terminal Group1 rekey
failures through this same one-way hardware retirement. The native TX
availability notification now requires a still-running radio/channel/queue;
it cannot advertise a newly quarantined ring. A separate EDMA/IRQ fault
explicitly latches `keyCache_.requiresReset`, including OS-owned WPA2 where
no driver supplicant callback exists. The source-only
`test_native_link_retirement_wiring.py` checks these exact call paths; it
does not establish that a physical AR9565 actually stopped or wiped keys.

Early native authentication/association/EAPOL reply submission failure
now enters the same terminal hardware retirement path. In particular an M4
submission that fails after pairwise/group key installation cannot merely
reset software station state while leaving automatic MAC responses, EDMA and
key RAM live. This closes a source-level path only; it does not enable a
post-reset reconnect or establish actual hardware queue isolation.

Implement one lifecycle owner joining these actual boundaries in order:
PCI provider/gate/D0 -> validated board -> reset/calibrated radio -> mapped
RX/TX/status queues -> IRQ work -> MAC/RSN keys -> native AirPort packets.
For each transition list its call site, owning context, generation, resource
transfer, cancellation and rollback. Add the reverse teardown path before
publishing the service. Test only material transitions with fakes as useful.

Trace an OS packet through encryption/queue completion and a received beacon
or protected packet back to the OS. Trace scan -> selection -> auth -> assoc
-> WPA2 -> controlled data port. Then trace provider loss and sleep/resume,
including key erasure, DMA isolation and a fresh reconnect. Correct buffer
capacity/retry semantics must survive failure between each pair of steps.

Do not flip implementationComplete/verification booleans or preprocessor flags
to manufacture completion. Required normal paths must exist and be reachable;
runtime safety checks remain. Keep this task open until every required callback
has a real source implementation and the Xcode target selects that path.

## Required ownership map to implement


- **Resource/state:** PCI provider and active MMIO capability
  - **Producer and consumer:** Serialized native owner -> NVM/reset/queue
    clients
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Revoke epoch before D0 loss, unmap or
    close

- **Resource/state:** RX DMA packet
  - **Producer and consumer:** Device -> completion owner -> MAC/native packet
    input
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Preserve backing until completion and
    every consumer releases it

- **Resource/state:** Scan/BSS result
  - **Producer and consumer:** Bounded parser/cache -> completed snapshot ->
    native result copy
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Ticket/connection generation; never a
    borrowed mutable DMA pointer

- **Resource/state:** EAPOL and key state
  - **Producer and consumer:** Explicit single supplicant owner -> keycache/data
    gate
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Reject stale connection work;
    quiesce/remove keys before context release

- **Resource/state:** TX OS packet
  - **Producer and consumer:** Native queue -> MAC/security -> device queue
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Completion/drop/cancel must resolve
    ownership exactly once

- **Resource/state:** Calibration/channel/power permission
  - **Producer and consumer:** Radio/board/country owner -> every TX path
  - **Retirement/invalidation boundary:** Invalidate on reset, channel/country
    change, RF-kill and power loss


Resolve whether native AirPort/OS or the portable supplicant owns each RSN
step; never let both independently run a handshake or program competing keys.
Keep DHCP/DNS/TCP in the OS networking stack, reached through the real native
packet interface. Do not implement another IP stack inside this kext.

## Required execution-context map to implement


- **Entry/context:** Primary interrupt filter, if selected
  - **Permitted work and cancellation contract:** Minimal interrupt-safe
    status/mask/defer operations; no blocking, allocation or command-gate entry.
    Mapping and callback owner stay retained through delivery drain

- **Entry/context:** Work-loop action / command-gated action
  - **Permitted work and cancellation contract:** Serialize epochs, queue
    ownership and short state transitions. A command-gate action can run on the
    caller's thread under the work-loop gate; thread identity alone does not
    prove serialization

- **Entry/context:** Sleepable allocation/NVM/reset worker
  - **Permitted work and cancellation contract:** Retain operation/provider
    resources, obey one register owner and a bounded cancellation/deadline
    contract; return under the gate to revalidate epoch before publishing output

- **Entry/context:** Native packet/control callback
  - **Permitted work and cancellation contract:** Copy or retain inputs
    according to the verified OS contract; validate connection/scan generation
    and transfer ownership once. Never retain borrowed request or RX storage
    past its valid lifetime

- **Entry/context:** Stop / sleep / termination
  - **Permitted work and cancellation contract:** Reject new work and revoke
    epochs, quiesce/isolate device access, cancel/drain outstanding operations
    and deferred callbacks, then release mappings/packets/provider. Never wait
    under a gate needed by the operation being drained


Bind these rules to actual functions and locks; the table alone is not an
implementation. Account for command-gate reentrancy and every call into an OS
client that could synchronously call back. Do not invoke external callbacks
while holding a private lock unless the verified API explicitly requires it.
Document lock order, sleep eligibility and power acknowledgements for the
selected Tahoe family rather than inferring them from portable fake tests.

Primary source: [IOCommandGate
semantics](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu),
[interrupt-filter
context](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu),
and [IOService
lifecycle](https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu).

