## Driver-owned WPA2 controlled-port staging — 2026-11-10

The native WCL V2 connect callback supplies a PMK to
`WifiEngine::chooseNetworkForDriverSupplicant`, **not** to the separately
retained OS-owned CCMP-key callback. The previous physical controlled-port
callback only admitted Open station state and could never admit a completed
native driver-owned WPA2 handshake. This is a real source integration mismatch,
not a reason to claim an on-air handshake or bypass the RF gate.

The native PCI owner's driver-owned PTK/GTK hardware installs now also bind
software CCMP TX/RX sessions to the same physical epoch, AP/station identities
and MIC-authenticated GTK RSC replay floor. Any failed mirror/install
quarantines the keycache; key removal revokes the mirrors and readiness proofs.
A deferred M4 is identified by its exact EDMA completion token, radio/join/key
epoch and successful hardware TX status. A standalone source-used security
predicate admits driver-owned WPA2 only with a complete keycache+software key
set, that M4 completion, real CCMP MIC/PN-verified data RX, and every existing
same-epoch RF, BSSID, beacon, queue, regulatory and TPC gate. Unsupported
legacy/enterprise/WPA3 and incompatible OS-owned key states remain closed.

The driver-owned receive route now checks protected MPDUs through the native
software CCMP verifier rather than the engine's cleartext data parser. It
never forwards protected EAPOL to a second supplicant. An authenticated CCMP
Group1 rekey now has a **source-wired driver-owned Group2 reply**:
CCMP-verified Group1 is consumed only once, its GTK replacement enters a
physically isolated native keycache lease, and a protected Group2 is deferred
until RX DMA unwinds. Separate M4/Group2 EDMA completion tokens prevent rekey
success from granting port readiness; failed Group2 submission/completion
revokes the connection. A synthetic C++14/C++17 sanitized fixture tests
Group2 MIC/sequence/replay. The integration is untested on real RF and does
not claim an operational native rekey.

The AR9565 hardware keycache may already decrypt/transform RX frames; the native
software verifier does **not** assume unmodified ciphertext and will refuse
admission if it cannot independently verify the received MIC/PN. This device
format/crypto-ownership interlock, atomic Group1/Group2 handling, real RF
activation, country/channel/TPC authority, and exact Tahoe runtime remain
P0 blockers. Source syntax/host fixtures cannot prove physical connectivity.
